  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "section-1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Description of the Selected Time Series and Their Expected Relationship"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "code-1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Main Series - New Olim by Month between 01.1989 - 05.2026 : \n",
    "A breakdown of the number of immigrants (Olim) arriving in Israel from January 1989 to May 2026, comprising 449 monthly observations. The series allows us to examine changes in immigration over time.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Exogenic Series - Israel's Geopolitical Risk Index (GPR)\n",
    "The exogenous series is Israel's country-specific Geopolitical Risk Index (GPR), developed by Caldara and Iacoviello. The index is based on the monthly share of newspaper articles discussing geopolitical threats, tensions, and events that mention Israel or its major cities. Higher index values indicate greater reported geopolitical risk associated with Israel.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Expected Relationship : \n",
    "We hypothesize a negative relationship between geopolitical risk in Israel and the number of new immigrants arriving in the country. During periods of heightened security tensions or geopolitical instability, Israel may be perceived as a less attractive destination for immigration, potentially causing prospective immigrants to postpone their arrival. However, immigration decisions are complex and influenced by various factors. Therefore, changes in geopolitical risk may affect immigration with a lag of several months rather than immediately. Additionally, geopolitical events in immigrants' countries of origin may also influence immigration patterns.\n",
   ]
  },
